Using the relativistic velocity‑addition formula  

\[
v_{\text{lab}}=\frac{v_{\text{water}}+u}{1+\dfrac{v_{\text{water}}u}{c^{2}}}
\]

where  

* \(u = \tfrac12c\) is the speed of the water in the lab,  
* \(v_{\text{water}} = \dfrac{c}{n}= \dfrac{c}{4/3}= \tfrac34c\) is the speed of light in the water’s rest frame.

\[
\begin{aligned}
v_{\text{lab}} &= \frac{\tfrac34c + \tfrac12c}{1+\frac{(\tfrac34c)(\tfrac12c)}{c^{2}}}
= \frac{\tfrac54c}{1+\tfrac{3}{8}}
= \frac{\tfrac54c}{\tfrac{11}{8}}
= \frac{5}{4}\cdot\frac{8}{11}c
= \frac{10}{11}c .
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus the light’s speed relative to the lab frame is \(\displaystyle \frac{10}{11}c\).

Answer: D
